From: XI14 <https://github.com/DavidFB-creator/MimOS>
Subject: [PATCH] users: put the page inside a scroll area

At 200% display scaling the users page does not fit the space it is given, and
until now the part that did not fit was simply unreachable: the confirm-password
field ran past the right edge of the pane with its status icon painted on top of
it, and the sidebar's last step scrolled out of a column it no longer fits.

Two previous attempts treated this as an arithmetic problem and reasoned about
how much room the row needed. Both were wrong in the same way -- they assumed
there was room to be found. The measured numbers say there is not. The test
display is 1024x768 physical, so at 200% the whole window is 512x384 device
independent pixels and the right-hand pane is roughly 262 of them wide. That is
narrower than a phone. No arrangement of a credential form fits a two-column row
into 262dip, and no arrangement fits this page's height into 384dip either.

ADR-062 rejected the second attempt for exactly this reason: stacking the two
password fields solved the horizontal overflow and produced a vertical one,
because the page then needed more than the window's height. The horizontal
overflow was traded for a vertical one. Neither can be arithmetic'd away.

A scroll area is the fix for a page that genuinely needs more space than exists.
The page keeps its natural minimum size, the viewport gives it whatever room the
pane has, and anything beyond that becomes scrollable rather than clipped.
Nothing overlaps, nothing is drawn outside the pane, and every field remains
reachable by keyboard and by pointer.

The scroll area is created with setWidgetResizable( true ) so the page still
expands to fill the pane at scales where it fits, which is every scale the
matrix measures except 200%. At 100% and 150% the scrollbars do not appear and
the page is unchanged. That is the falsifiable part: if the 100% or 150%
captures differ from the previous image, this patch did something it should not.

The frame is removed because the pane already provides the visual boundary, and
a sunken frame inside a themed page would read as a second panel.

The scroll area is given an object name so a stylesheet can address it and its
children exactly. Without one the only handle is the QScrollArea type, and how far
a type selector reaches into a scroll area depends on which of the widgets
involved honour a stylesheet background: a QFrame does, a plain QWidget subclass
does not unless it carries WA_StyledBackground, and the viewport is a separate
child of the scroll area rather than of the page. That question cannot be settled
by reading the class hierarchy, and finding out from a composed image costs a
forty-minute cycle. An object name removes the question instead of answering it.

UsersViewStep now owns the scroll area rather than the page, so the destructor
guard moves with it: the scroll area is the widget handed to the view manager,
and it is the one that may or may not have been reparented. Deleting it deletes
the page it owns.

Reported upstream by MimOS (XI14). See ADR-050, ADR-059 and ADR-062 in that
project.
---
--- a/src/modules/users/UsersViewStep.h
+++ b/src/modules/users/UsersViewStep.h
@@ -18,6 +18,8 @@
 #include <QObject>
 #include <QVariant>
 
+class QScrollArea;
+
 class Config;
 class UsersPage;
 
@@ -47,6 +49,7 @@
     void setConfigurationMap( const QVariantMap& configurationMap ) override;
 
 private:
+    QScrollArea* m_scroll;
     UsersPage* m_widget;
     Config* m_config;
 };
--- a/src/modules/users/UsersViewStep.cpp
+++ b/src/modules/users/UsersViewStep.cpp
@@ -20,10 +20,13 @@
 #include "utils/NamedEnum.h"
 #include "utils/Variant.h"
 
+#include <QScrollArea>
+
 CALAMARES_PLUGIN_FACTORY_DEFINITION( UsersViewStepFactory, registerPlugin< UsersViewStep >(); )
 
 UsersViewStep::UsersViewStep( QObject* parent )
     : Calamares::ViewStep( parent )
+    , m_scroll( nullptr )
     , m_widget( nullptr )
     , m_config( new Config( this ) )
 {
@@ -35,9 +38,9 @@
 
 UsersViewStep::~UsersViewStep()
 {
-    if ( m_widget && m_widget->parent() == nullptr )
+    if ( m_scroll && m_scroll->parent() == nullptr )
     {
-        m_widget->deleteLater();
+        m_scroll->deleteLater();
     }
 }
 
@@ -52,11 +55,22 @@
 QWidget*
 UsersViewStep::widget()
 {
-    if ( !m_widget )
+    if ( !m_scroll )
     {
         m_widget = new UsersPage( m_config );
+
+        // At 200% scaling on a 1024x768 display this page has about 262
+        // device-independent pixels of width and 384 of height to work
+        // with. It does not fit, and without a scroll area the part that
+        // does not fit is drawn outside the pane or on top of another
+        // widget. See ADR-062.
+        m_scroll = new QScrollArea();
+        m_scroll->setObjectName( "usersScrollArea" );
+        m_scroll->setWidgetResizable( true );
+        m_scroll->setFrameShape( QFrame::NoFrame );
+        m_scroll->setWidget( m_widget );
     }
-    return m_widget;
+    return m_scroll;
 }
 
 
